ent-BookAshWalkerGuide = ash walker's guide
    .desc = A worn book bound in dark hide. Notes on hunting, husbandry and the tribe's rites share its smoke-stained pages.

ash-walker-guide-book-content =
    { "[" }bold]AN ASH WALKER'S GUIDE[/bold]

    Check your weapon before leaving. Take water and dressings, share your catch, and tend your wounds before the next hunt.
    Recipes for weapons, clothing and tools are in the hunter's craft book. These pages explain how to use them and keep the settlement fed.

    { "[" }bold]Hunting and butchering[/bold]
    Decide whether a carcass is for butchering, the nest or a rune. Offerings must be whole. The obelisk will not take a butchered beast.
    A knife from the hunting crate will earn your first bones and sinews; a bone dagger makes the work faster. A watcher yields one bone and two sinews. A goliath yields two bones, two to four pieces of hide and two cuts of meat. A legion leaves two bones; examine its other remains separately.
    Tribal hunters know the weak spots of the local beasts. Hold an axe in both hands for a stronger blow. A bow also needs both hands; recover unbroken arrows for another hunt.
    Tribal armor fits ash walkers and cannot be worn by outsiders. A hide cloak takes the place of outer armor. Remove your helmet before raising its hood. Hide softens heat and blows, but will not save you in lava.
    Keep knives, arrows, food, dressings and small tools on your belt. A heavy axe can hang from your back or the outside of your cloak.

    { "[" }bold]The nest and spring[/bold]
    Bring a whole carcass to the obelisk and make an offering. You can also drag the body directly to it. Leave room around the nest for the tribe's new eggs. A destroyed nest will grow no more young.
    The stone spring replenishes its clean water. Fill a flask for hunting, bowls for remedies and a bucket for watering the beds.

    { "[" }bold]Mushrooms and growing beds[/bold]
    Polypore supplies fibers for cloth, boards and utensils. Porcini leaves make food and dressings. Never eat raw inocybe: roasting removes its poison. Ember caps glow and give iron-rich juice. Cactus fruit quenches thirst and its pulp soothes burns.
    Gather ripe produce by hand. Mushrooms and cacti grow back if left rooted. Sieve some fresh produce for spores or seeds to plant in ash beds.
    Water the beds from the spring and fertilize them with surplus produce. Remove weeds with a hoe and dead plants with a trowel. An ember-cap lantern needs no fuel.

    { "[" }bold]The hearth and leatherworking[/bold]
    Feed the hearth with wood or fungal boards. One board burns for two minutes; the hearth holds up to ten minutes of fuel.
    Lay food on top. There is room for four items: meat, porcini leaves, inocybe caps or fish. To make leather, scrape a piece of goliath hide and dry it over the coals. One piece of raw hide makes one piece of leather.
    Grind fresh mushrooms and fruit in a mortar, then pour the juice into a bowl. A mushroom bowl holds 50 units of liquid.

    { "[" }bold]Tending wounds[/bold]
    Use fungal dressings for bruises, cuts and punctures, and to stop bleeding. Cactus dressings treat burns from heat and acid.
    After blood loss, bind the wounds before sipping blood tonic. It replaces blood but does not close wounds. Let one dose pass before drinking another: 20 units in the body will poison you.

    { "[" }bold]Building the settlement[/bold]
    Wield a pickaxe in both hands to break basalt boulders. Each yields 12 to 16 blocks. Build walls and gates around pens and homes; the gates need no electricity.
    Lay basalt slabs on bare stone or earth. Pry up an existing floor before replacing it. Removed slabs can be used again.

    { "[" }bold]Gutlunches[/bold]
    Keep a female and a small red male in a pen. They cannot open gates, and neither lava nor ash storms trouble them.
    Feed them raw butchered meat by hand or leave it nearby on the ground. They will not eat whole carcasses or cooked food.
    Each piece is eaten whole. Richer meat produces more milk; cutting it smaller produces no extra. A full milk sac turns pale and holds 50 units. Save large cuts for animals with room for them.
    Both sexes give milk. Hold an open bowl or bucket beneath one for five seconds. Their milk nourishes, quenches thirst and helps replace lost blood. They need more meat to produce more milk.
    For breeding, leave the female a full sac and the male at least half, and keep them close together. The female spends all her milk and the male spends 25 units. A young gutlunch is born after two minutes and grows up in another three.
    Keep no more than six animals within 32 meters, including unborn young. Youngsters cannot breed. Gutlunches are not accepted as offerings, and butchering a youngster yields less meat than raising it consumed. Protect your breeding pair.

    { "[" }bold]Crossing lava[/bold]
    Touch the boat with an oar to board or step ashore. Keep an oar in either hand while rowing. Boats travel on lava and must be dragged across land. You can grab the hull from two meters away. Its lining smothers flames on you as you board.
    On land, open the cargo net, leave your catch beside the boat and fasten the net. It holds twelve items or bodies, but no living passengers. You cannot row with the net open.
    Pull up to a clear shore before disembarking. You cannot step out in the middle of a lake. The hull protects a seated rider from lava, but not from attacks or fireballs. If it breaks offshore, rider and cargo fall into the lava. A boat cannot cross a chasm.

    { "[" }bold]Great hunts[/bold]
    Search the battle site after an arena guardian falls. One special trophy will lie among the spoils. Craft with the trophy; the guardian's body is not needed.
    A drake spike makes armor against heat and heavy blows. It slightly restricts movement; the armor and hood fit only ash walkers.
    A colossus carapace makes a shield. Carry it in hand and watch its condition: repeated blows will break it. A hierophant crystal belongs in a two-handed hammer that can throw an enemy back. Mind where the carcass will land.
    Bubblegum's claws make a quick blade for deep wounds and butchering. Each trophy makes one item, so choose what the tribe needs most.

    { "[" }bold]Runes and offerings[/bold]
    Use a bone dagger or blood claw to carve a rune into clear, solid ground on your home Lavaland. Carving takes four seconds. The same blade can erase the sign.
    Leave offerings within one and a half meters of the rune. Drag a carcass to the sign or touch the rune to begin. Stay still and avoid being struck. An interrupted rite leaves its offerings intact.

    { "[" }bold]Hunt[/bold]
    Offer a whole goliath. The rite takes eight seconds. Gather tribesmates within two meters of the sign, with no walls between them and the rune. Their blows against local beasts become stronger for four minutes. Repeating the rite does not strengthen an existing blessing.

    { "[" }bold]Mending[/bold]
    Offer any whole watcher and gather the wounded nearby. After eight seconds, the rite is complete. Over the next three minutes, bruises, cuts and burns gradually heal. One offering has limited strength. Blessings do not add together; treat poison and blood loss with remedies and dressings. This rite cannot raise the dead.

    { "[" }bold]Return[/bold]
    Lay a fallen tribesmate beside the sign. Leave two whole goliaths, basilisks or watchers nearby, and an open bowl holding 10 units of blood tonic. A pale lava fish can replace one carcass.
    The rite takes twenty seconds. The spirit then has thirty seconds to answer. If it refuses or the body is carried away, the offerings remain.
    The returned hunter remains wounded and weak for a minute. Tend to them at once. Each fallen tribesmate can be brought back by this rite only once.

    { "[" }bold]Fishing in lava[/bold]
    A bone rod already has a hook and heatproof line. Any ordinary food can serve as bait: meat, fish, mushrooms, fruit or a prepared meal. Open sealed packaging first.
    Apply food to the rod to set aside a portion of bait. One piece may provide several portions. A rod holds five, and each cast uses one.
    Fish from shore or a stationary boat. Cast no farther than four meters. When something bites, you have three seconds to set the hook. Then work the line, tightening and easing it as the catch pulls. Fish struggle harder than sunken objects.
    Retrieve the catch after landing it. If your hands are full, it stays on the rod until you free a hand and take it. Cook ordinary fish over the hearth; save pale fish for a Return rite if needed.
    Besides eels and armored fish, the line may bring up materials, tools, medicine, trinkets and junk. Valuable finds are rare. Move on when the bites stop; the old spot needs time to recover.
    Stay still and keep the rod in the same hand while fishing. Reel in before leaving. A lost catch takes the bait, but you keep the rod.

ash-walker-recipe-book-content =
    { "[" }bold]THE HUNTER'S CRAFT BOOK[/bold]

    Lay your materials within reach. These crafts need no workbench. The ash walker's guide explains how to gather supplies, tend the settlement and perform rites.
    Sinew means watcher sinew; raw hide and leather come from goliaths. Count individual pieces, not bundles. Fibers come from polypore. Recipes produce one item unless stated otherwise.

    { "[" }bold]Weapons[/bold]
    Bone dagger — 2 bones.
    Sinew-bound bone spear — 4 bones and 1 sinew.
    Bone axe — 6 bones and 3 sinews.
    Bone shortbow — 4 bones and 2 sinews.
    Bone arrow — 1 bone.

    { "[" }bold]Armor and clothing[/bold]
    Ash walker bone armor — 6 bones.
    Ash walker bone helmet — 4 bones.
    Bone bracers — 2 bones and 1 sinew.
    Bone talisman — 2 bones and 1 sinew.
    Skull loincloth — 2 bones and 1 piece of leather.
    Hooded goliath cloak — 2 raw hides, 1 piece of leather and 2 sinews.

    { "[" }bold]Bags and supplies[/bold]
    Sinew restraints — 1 sinew.
    Hunter's belt — 1 piece of leather and 2 sinews.
    Leather backpack — 2 pieces of leather and 2 sinews.

    { "[" }bold]Fiber and leather[/bold]
    Pressed fungal boards, 3 — 2 portions of fiber.
    Fungal cloth, 2 pieces — 2 portions of fiber.
    Leather — scrape 1 raw hide and dry it on a fueled hearth.

    { "[" }bold]Utensils and growing beds[/bold]
    Spore sieve — 1 bone and 1 portion of fiber.
    Ash growing bed — 2 basalt blocks and 1 portion of fiber.
    Ember-cap lantern — 1 ember stem and 1 portion of fiber.
    Mushroom bowl — 2 portions of fiber.
    Basalt mortar — 2 basalt blocks.
    Stone hearth — 6 basalt blocks and 2 bones.

    { "[" }bold]Dressings and remedies[/bold]
    Fungal dressing — 1 porcini leaf and 1 portion of fiber.
    Cactus dressing — 1 cactus fruit and 1 porcini leaf.
    Blood tonic — grind an ember stem and a cactus fruit, then mix their juices in a bowl. The ratio is 1 part iron, 1 part vitamin and 2 parts water.
    Roasted porcini leaf — place a raw leaf on a fueled hearth.
    Roasted inocybe cap — place a raw cap on a fueled hearth and wait until cooked. Raw inocybe is poisonous.

    { "[" }bold]Stone buildings[/bold]
    Basalt wall — 4 basalt blocks.
    Basalt gate — 6 basalt blocks and 2 sinews.
    Basalt floor slabs, 4 — 1 basalt block.

    { "[" }bold]Boat and fishing rod[/bold]
    Hide boat — 3 raw hides.
    Bone oar — 2 bones.
    Bone fishing rod — 3 bones, 2 sinews and 1 raw hide.

    { "[" }bold]Trophy equipment[/bold]
    Drake armor — 1 drake spike, 4 raw hides and 2 sinews.
    Colossus shield — 1 colossus carapace, 6 bones and 2 sinews.
    Resonant hammer — 1 hierophant crystal, 6 bones and 2 sinews.
    Blood claw — 1 Bubblegum claw trophy, 4 bones and 2 sinews.
